1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:01,633
An impossible dream.

2
00:00:01,634 --> 00:00:08,000
Connecting two oceans by digging a canal through dense jungle and savage heat.

3
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:13,632
Poisonous snakes and malarial mosquitoes. It killed tens of thousands.

4
00:00:13,633 --> 00:00:15,000
It cost billions.

5
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:17,095
It nearly toppled the government.

6
00:00:17,096 --> 00:00:23,000
But in the end, it was a technological triumph that touched every aspect of our modern lives.

7
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:27,000
The Panama Canal, next on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:58,780 --> 00:00:59,980
I'm out.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,820
Early explorers feared that the very edge of the world lay in the uncharted waters of the Atlantic.

10
00:01:08,660 --> 00:01:11,820
That there was a great abyss that would swallow ships whole,

11
00:01:12,460 --> 00:01:15,960
plunging men into a living hell of nightmares and mythic monsters.

12
00:01:21,540 --> 00:01:27,880
So when Columbus stumbled onto the New World when he came to places like Panama in 1502,

13
00:01:27,881 --> 00:01:32,040
he was surprised to discover seeming paradise.

14
00:01:33,800 --> 00:01:35,280
Seeming paradise.

15
00:01:36,560 --> 00:01:39,640
But in a way, the old stories were true,

16
00:01:40,100 --> 00:01:42,540
for there were monsters in this paradise.

17
00:01:43,400 --> 00:01:47,060
There were frustration, disease, and broken dreams.

18
00:01:48,560 --> 00:01:53,760
And conquering those would be one of the greatest achievements of the 20th century.

19
00:01:53,761 --> 00:02:00,760
It started with the incredible notion of challenging the planet's natural balance by linking the two greatest oceans on Earth,

20
00:02:00,761 --> 00:02:08,760
to slice a waterway across the continental divide of Panama and bypass the navigational gauntlet of South America.

21
00:02:08,761 --> 00:02:12,707
There was more at stake than improved shipping lanes.

22
00:02:12,708 --> 00:02:15,760
This would be Mother Nature's last stand.

23
00:02:15,761 --> 00:02:20,760
People would have to declare dominion over the very form and function of the land.

24
00:02:20,761 --> 00:02:30,520
It was the most important engineering project of its time, involving the largest earthen dam ever built, the largest man-made lake ever created.

25
00:02:31,160 --> 00:02:42,280
The epic effort was accomplished only through the grueling labor of 100,000 workers, whipped by the ambitions of powerful men who enjoyed breaking all the rules.

26
00:02:50,760 --> 00:02:57,760
Panama has always attracted explorers and adventurers.

27
00:02:57,761 --> 00:03:07,760
In 1534, Spanish engineers surveyed the isthmus and proposed a plan for a canal that would

28
00:03:07,761 --> 00:03:11,760
link the Atlantic and Pacific Oceans.

29
00:03:11,761 --> 00:03:15,760
It was a bold idea that was quickly discarded.

30
00:03:15,761 --> 00:03:23,120
The jungle was too dense, the weather too oppressive, the task too enormous for the limited technology of the time.

31
00:03:23,840 --> 00:03:30,360
But a seed had been planted in the imagination of explorers which would bear bitter fruit for the next three and a half centuries.

32
00:03:32,940 --> 00:03:35,780
Ambitious projects demand ambitious men.

33
00:03:35,781 --> 00:03:38,780
There was nothing meek about Ferdinand de Lesseps,

34
00:03:38,781 --> 00:03:41,780
a celebrated entrepreneur who promoted the French effort

35
00:03:41,781 --> 00:03:46,780
to carve the 105-mile Suez Canal in 1869.

36
00:03:47,780 --> 00:03:51,780
De Lesseps' celebrated success in the Middle East was the pride of France.

37
00:03:51,781 --> 00:03:57,780
So 10 years later, when he focused on building a canal to span the 50-mile width of Panama,

38
00:03:57,781 --> 00:04:00,780
there was no doubt that he would again succeed.

39
00:04:00,781 --> 00:04:03,780
But there were two problems with his plan.

40
00:04:03,781 --> 00:04:05,580
with his plan.

41
00:04:05,581 --> 00:04:09,300
One, de Lesseps was a great promoter, not a great engineer.

42
00:04:09,301 --> 00:04:11,040
Two, he had never been to Panama.

43
00:04:16,680 --> 00:04:20,920
Unlike the Suez, Panama was a savage tropical terrain,

44
00:04:20,921 --> 00:04:23,580
a swampy, overgrown land that would not easily

45
00:04:23,581 --> 00:04:25,020
bend to the will of man.

46
00:04:28,120 --> 00:04:29,860
But with the support of the French people

47
00:04:29,861 --> 00:04:32,140
and millions of dollars in investment capital,

48
00:04:32,141 --> 00:04:37,640
De Lesseps and his team of eager young men began their nine-year exercise in futility.

49
00:04:39,760 --> 00:04:41,413
Problems began immediately.

50
00:04:41,414 --> 00:04:45,700
Due to an eight-month rainy season, the jungle grows in an outstanding

51
00:04:45,701 --> 00:04:50,922
rate with razor-sharp grasses and ferns which easily cut through the skin.

52
00:04:50,923 --> 00:04:51,980
This all had to

53
00:04:51,981 --> 00:04:53,897
be cleared by hand.

54
00:04:53,898 --> 00:05:01,060
And the jungle was home to a savage variety of insects, spiders, ticks,

55
00:05:01,061 --> 00:05:06,220
mosquitoes, poisonous snakes, the French engineers and their laborers from local

56
00:05:06,221 --> 00:05:12,440
Caribbean islands were dying in rapid fashion. 75% of all workers who entered

57
00:05:12,441 --> 00:05:17,500
the hospital for treatment of smallpox, typhoid, yellow fever, food poisoning or

58
00:05:17,501 --> 00:05:26,800
snakebite died painfully, prematurely. Most of the illness was blamed on

59
00:05:26,801 --> 00:05:32,980
malaria, which the French believed was caused by deadly vapors seeping from the rotting jungle.

60
00:05:34,180 --> 00:05:39,320
Despite solid medical evidence, the French refused to accept the fact that many tropical

61
00:05:39,321 --> 00:05:43,049
diseases are carried primarily by mosquitoes.

62
00:05:43,050 --> 00:05:46,280
De Lesseps and the French had the will,

63
00:05:46,700 --> 00:05:51,760
but not the way. Their plan to carve a sea-level canal had never been practical.

64
00:05:51,761 --> 00:05:56,520
The unstable earth of the Panamanian Mountains is prone to sudden slides

65
00:05:56,521 --> 00:06:00,240
and could never be tamed by the primitive French construction equipment.

66
00:06:01,240 --> 00:06:04,620
By 1889, the Europeans had run out of money.

67
00:06:05,080 --> 00:06:10,060
They left behind rusting hardware and the corpses of 20,000 men.

68
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:15,940
Those who survived were credited with causing the worst financial failure of its time.

69
00:06:15,941 --> 00:06:21,980
A $300 million catastrophe which nearly toppled the French government.

70
00:06:22,720 --> 00:06:27,120
But the dream of building a Panama Canal was not dead.

71
00:06:30,060 --> 00:06:35,040
Every man had a job to do, and he was to do it to the best of his ability.

72
00:06:35,680 --> 00:06:39,280
Because the French had failed,

73
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:45,840
and we were determined to make a success of building the canal.

74
00:06:45,940 --> 00:06:51,800
not only for ourselves but for the United States government

75
00:06:51,801 --> 00:06:53,560
and for the president.

76
00:06:56,340 --> 00:07:00,620
At 104 years of age, Robert Dill was the last member

77
00:07:00,621 --> 00:07:03,520
of the American team that built the Panama Canal.

78
00:07:04,380 --> 00:07:07,300
When he arrived on the Isthmus in 1911,

79
00:07:07,440 --> 00:07:10,760
the canal symbolized America's emergence as a world power,

80
00:07:11,080 --> 00:07:15,300
and President Theodore Roosevelt was the embodiment of that spirit.

81
00:07:15,940 --> 00:07:20,580
Teddy's advice was to speak softly and carry a big stick,

82
00:07:20,581 --> 00:07:22,980
but he never spoke softly.

83
00:07:25,220 --> 00:07:28,940
His mythic image had been forged in 1898

84
00:07:28,941 --> 00:07:31,000
during the Spanish-American War

85
00:07:31,001 --> 00:07:33,100
when, as Assistant Secretary of the Navy,

86
00:07:33,540 --> 00:07:37,440
he had led the Rough Riders on a bloody charge up San Juan Hill.

87
00:07:38,480 --> 00:07:40,600
The public applauded the victory,

88
00:07:40,780 --> 00:07:43,560
but Teddy was upset that badly needed support

89
00:07:43,561 --> 00:07:48,440
from West Coast battleships was not available for his Caribbean campaign.

90
00:07:50,460 --> 00:07:56,240
He saw an immediate strategic need for a canal that would facilitate a two-ocean navy.

91
00:07:58,320 --> 00:08:04,020
After the fighting, Teddy became vice president on the winning Republican presidential ticket in 1900.

92
00:08:05,200 --> 00:08:10,760
A year later, when William McKinley was shot to death by an assassin, Roosevelt became president.

93
00:08:13,560 --> 00:08:21,200
His first order of business was the Panama Canal.

94
00:08:22,840 --> 00:08:26,320
Newsreels acclaimed his early addresses to Congress where he declared,

95
00:08:26,321 --> 00:08:34,260
no work which remains to be undertaken on this continent is of such consequence to the American people as the Panama Canal.

96
00:08:37,740 --> 00:08:41,860
The Isthmus of Panama was the northern province of Colombia,

97
00:08:41,861 --> 00:08:46,720
and Colombia had no interest in granting the U.S. the right to build a canal.

98
00:08:47,380 --> 00:08:51,240
So Roosevelt encouraged a handful of prominent Panamanians

99
00:08:51,241 --> 00:08:56,040
who started a revolution in 1903 and declared the region independent.

100
00:08:56,840 --> 00:08:59,500
The bloodless coup took less than 24 hours.

101
00:09:00,360 --> 00:09:01,920
And to be certain it wouldn't fail,

102
00:09:02,080 --> 00:09:04,240
Teddy sent U.S. battleships to the isthmus

103
00:09:04,241 --> 00:09:08,720
with orders to shoot down any Colombian efforts to keep its territory.

104
00:09:08,721 --> 00:09:16,600
Roosevelt denied any involvement with the revolution until relentless questions from reporters provoked him into saying,

105
00:09:16,601 --> 00:09:21,720
I took the isthmus. Those four words were to prove costly.

106
00:09:22,660 --> 00:09:28,200
Colombia demanded $25 million from the United States as reparations for the illegal seizure.

107
00:09:29,320 --> 00:09:37,700
But Teddy could finally build his canal, thanks to a hastily drawn agreement in which the United States would give the new nation of Panama

108
00:09:37,701 --> 00:09:40,880
$10 million for the right to build and operate

109
00:09:40,881 --> 00:09:42,780
a transcontinental waterway.

110
00:09:43,740 --> 00:09:46,920
No Panamanians ever signed that agreement.

111
00:09:47,680 --> 00:09:51,280
Still, preparations for building the dam began.

112
00:09:53,980 --> 00:09:59,280
In 1905, John Stevens became the chief engineer of the project.

113
00:10:00,060 --> 00:10:03,180
I would say that Stevens was really the architect

114
00:10:03,181 --> 00:10:07,680
because when we took over the building of the canal in 1940,

115
00:10:07,700 --> 00:10:17,140
they still didn't know whether there was going to be a sea level canal or just what they were going to do.

116
00:10:18,480 --> 00:10:26,420
Stevens convinced Roosevelt of the plan to dam Panama's mightiest river and create what was then the world's largest man-made lake.

117
00:10:27,060 --> 00:10:35,340
Then use a system of locks to raise and lower ships 85 feet to the level of the lake for their transit across the continental divide.

118
00:10:35,341 --> 00:10:39,180
But problems still lingered.

119
00:10:39,181 --> 00:10:41,640
How would Stevens protect his workforce

120
00:10:41,641 --> 00:10:44,780
from the ravages of disease, which had already claimed

121
00:10:44,781 --> 00:10:47,480
20,000 lives during the French effort?

122
00:10:47,481 --> 00:10:53,400
The answer was a combination of cunning and common sense.

123
00:10:53,401 --> 00:10:55,500
When John Stevens addressed his workers,

124
00:10:55,501 --> 00:10:57,900
he inspired them with this speech.

125
00:10:57,901 --> 00:11:00,400
There are three diseases on the isthmus,

126
00:11:00,401 --> 00:11:03,340
yellow fever, malaria, and cold feet.

127
00:11:03,341 --> 00:11:06,640
and the worst disease of all is cold feet.

128
00:11:12,420 --> 00:11:16,420
At the turn of the century, Panama was a death trap.

129
00:11:17,220 --> 00:11:21,460
For Robert Dill, it was also an opportunity to forget the past.

130
00:11:22,100 --> 00:11:26,400
Because I learned that my fiancée

131
00:11:26,401 --> 00:11:31,000
had gone out with one of her former boyfriends,

132
00:11:31,001 --> 00:11:37,000
And so instead of joining the Foreign Legion or jumping off a bridge,

133
00:11:37,001 --> 00:11:42,000
I decided that I would go to Panama and work on the canal.

134
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:53,000
I lived in Gorgona in the bachelor quarters that were provided by the Canal Commission.

135
00:11:53,001 --> 00:12:02,440
There were two of us in one room, and the buildings were built for circulation of air.

136
00:12:02,441 --> 00:12:10,860
The aggravation, I might say, would be that due to the dampness or the humidity, we would

137
00:12:10,861 --> 00:12:17,000
get up in the morning and open our clothes closet, and we'd find that our shoes were

138
00:12:17,001 --> 00:12:19,920
full of mold.

139
00:12:19,921 --> 00:12:24,800
Most of the men who built the canal were not Americans assigned to bachelor quarters,

140
00:12:24,801 --> 00:12:30,120
but tens of thousands of desperately poor workers from impoverished Caribbean islands,

141
00:12:30,121 --> 00:12:36,380
who were housed under far more primitive living conditions and worked for far less money.

142
00:12:36,381 --> 00:12:43,280
The laborers paid 90 cents a day, and their meals were 9 cents.

143
00:12:43,281 --> 00:12:50,960
quarters were provided and the rest of course was up to them whether they saved it or spent it.

144
00:12:52,160 --> 00:12:57,280
Well my pay as machinists I think started at about 38 cents an hour

145
00:12:59,120 --> 00:13:07,520
and I received four promotions and in the five and a half years.

146
00:13:07,521 --> 00:13:15,120
There was only one thing equal between the Americans and the massive black labor force,

147
00:13:15,121 --> 00:13:18,880
the possibility that tropical diseases would claim their lives.

148
00:13:19,380 --> 00:13:23,360
It was a reality that Roosevelt had faced during the Spanish-American War.

149
00:13:24,860 --> 00:13:33,860
Thirteen times of the men that were killed during the battle had died from malaria fever,

150
00:13:33,861 --> 00:13:34,943
from disease.

151
00:13:34,944 --> 00:13:40,860
So when Tyler Roosevelt knew that we had a sanitation problem to solve,

152
00:13:40,861 --> 00:13:47,678
that Dr. Gargis was the man for the job.

153
00:13:47,679 --> 00:13:55,860
He was the man that eradicated the malaria fever

154
00:13:55,861 --> 00:13:59,760
and typhoid in Cuba.

155
00:14:01,160 --> 00:14:05,440
He felt that in addition to the health situation,

156
00:14:06,700 --> 00:14:09,040
we had to build streets

157
00:14:09,041 --> 00:14:12,380
because some of the streets were drainage dishes.

158
00:14:13,660 --> 00:14:16,680
And he called off all the work on the canal

159
00:14:16,681 --> 00:14:19,560
until he got in a sanitary condition

160
00:14:19,561 --> 00:14:22,020
to where the men then could feel free to work.

161
00:14:22,240 --> 00:14:23,480
That had to be done first.

162
00:14:25,860 --> 00:14:29,540
Drainage ditches were cleaned of standing water where mosquitoes breed.

163
00:14:30,100 --> 00:14:34,500
Swampgrass was trimmed to allow small fish to feast on the insect larvae.

164
00:14:35,320 --> 00:14:39,940
Ponds and puddles were sprayed with a film of oil to kill the newly hatched insects.

165
00:14:40,800 --> 00:14:45,180
Living quarters were fumigated and insect screens were attached to windows.

166
00:14:45,181 --> 00:14:51,880
As the environment was being controlled, massive shipments of sanitation materials arrived.

167
00:14:51,881 --> 00:15:01,360
120 tons of insecticide, 8,000 pounds of soap, thousands of buckets, brooms and rakes.

168
00:15:02,980 --> 00:15:06,420
In a few months, the men had accomplished the impossible.

169
00:15:07,140 --> 00:15:11,660
By Christmas of 1905, yellow fever had been eliminated in Panama.

170
00:15:15,460 --> 00:15:17,980
But health did not come cheaply.

171
00:15:17,981 --> 00:15:22,980
Every mosquito killed cost the United States government $10.

172
00:15:22,981 --> 00:15:26,980
But the government went even further to protect the workers.

173
00:15:26,981 --> 00:15:30,980
The men were prescribed regular rations of quinine.

174
00:15:30,981 --> 00:15:34,980
They would have a man with liquid quinine,

175
00:15:34,981 --> 00:15:41,980
the person that would go around to the shops, the gangs,

176
00:15:41,981 --> 00:15:44,980
wherever there was activity and men working,

177
00:15:44,981 --> 00:15:54,980
And you would take the required amount of liquid quinine.

178
00:15:54,981 --> 00:16:00,980
But quinine was distributed to workers in a racially segregated way.

179
00:16:00,981 --> 00:16:03,980
American men were labeled as gold employees,

180
00:16:03,981 --> 00:16:09,980
and the black Caribbean laborers as silver employees.

181
00:16:09,981 --> 00:16:19,020
And the gold would drink out of the gold cup, and the non-Americans, of course, would drink out of the silver cup.

182
00:16:19,860 --> 00:16:25,320
When canal construction began full force, new dangers surfaced.

183
00:16:26,160 --> 00:16:36,680
The hardest work was in the Calabra Cut, where whole mountains were removed to carve a path eight miles long, 300 feet wide, 50 feet deep.

184
00:16:36,681 --> 00:16:42,920
This man-made gorge would later be filled with the cooled waters dammed from the Chagras River.

185
00:16:43,800 --> 00:16:47,620
Until then, the men worked in 120-degree temperatures

186
00:16:47,621 --> 00:16:53,060
to blast away sliding hillsides and dig 200 trainloads of earth every day.

187
00:16:53,780 --> 00:16:57,220
50 to 60 steam shovels were operating at any one time

188
00:16:57,221 --> 00:17:00,580
in a choreographed dance with nonstop locomotives.

189
00:17:00,581 --> 00:17:05,320
So they arranged the timing that the train would move very slowly.

190
00:17:05,321 --> 00:17:09,320
The steam shovel would come over and put a load.

191
00:17:09,321 --> 00:17:14,320
And it would make a load and the train would move far enough then ahead

192
00:17:14,321 --> 00:17:16,320
for it to put the second dipper there.

193
00:17:16,321 --> 00:17:21,320
In other words, there was no lost motion by the steam shovels.

194
00:17:25,320 --> 00:17:30,320
Special dumping devices allowed the trains to be unloaded with one giant sweep,

195
00:17:30,321 --> 00:17:36,320
deposit millions of tons of soil to create a huge earthen dam one and a half miles long,

196
00:17:36,321 --> 00:17:39,320
a half mile thick at the base.

197
00:17:39,321 --> 00:17:45,320
It would take that much volume to hold back the water of the huge man-made lake.

198
00:17:47,320 --> 00:17:53,320
Meanwhile in the cut, 61 million pounds of dynamite was the primary tool for moving earth.

199
00:17:53,321 --> 00:17:59,320
The explosive force was equal to all the firepower used in all the wars the United States had fought

200
00:17:59,321 --> 00:18:00,820
up until that time.

201
00:18:06,520 --> 00:18:10,660
Often the tropical heat would make the dynamite unstable.

202
00:18:10,760 --> 00:18:13,160
It would explode unpredictably.

203
00:18:13,260 --> 00:18:18,080
A sudden blast killed 27 men in a single blinding instant.

204
00:18:18,180 --> 00:18:21,980
Other workers were killed or injured by flying debris.

205
00:18:22,080 --> 00:18:24,980
Funerals were as regular as rain.

206
00:18:24,981 --> 00:18:28,380
rain.

207
00:18:28,381 --> 00:18:31,260
Sometimes the blast sparked deadly fires.

208
00:18:31,261 --> 00:18:34,060
A Panamanian Fire Department was formed to respond,

209
00:18:34,061 --> 00:18:38,400
but was largely untrained in the techniques of fighting fires.

210
00:18:38,401 --> 00:18:42,500
The Panamanian Fire Department, instead of going around,

211
00:18:42,501 --> 00:18:46,300
you know, and fighting back it, they just followed with it.

212
00:18:46,301 --> 00:18:49,080
And when it exploded, I think there

213
00:18:49,081 --> 00:18:54,740
were six or seven firemen that were just blown to bits.

214
00:18:54,741 --> 00:19:00,140
It was not uncommon for workers to be ground under the wheels of the speeding trains,

215
00:19:00,141 --> 00:19:04,880
caught unaware due to the deafening noise at the site.

216
00:19:04,881 --> 00:19:08,720
But Teddy Roosevelt was pleased with the progress.

217
00:19:08,721 --> 00:19:16,160
The American effort was digging in a single day what the French had taken a month to match.

218
00:19:16,161 --> 00:19:23,800
In 1906, Roosevelt decided to see the work for himself and arrived in Panama for an inspection tour.

219
00:19:23,801 --> 00:19:25,640
The first time in history a president

220
00:19:25,641 --> 00:19:28,040
had left the United States while in office,

221
00:19:28,041 --> 00:19:31,060
and the first time a world leader had ever posed for photos

222
00:19:31,061 --> 00:19:33,880
in the seat of a steam shovel.

223
00:19:33,881 --> 00:19:38,800
It was in November, which was the worst month

224
00:19:38,801 --> 00:19:41,420
of the rainy season.

225
00:19:41,421 --> 00:19:44,600
And when he saw the conditions under which the men were

226
00:19:44,601 --> 00:19:49,880
working, their sincerity, their determination

227
00:19:49,881 --> 00:19:59,320
to get the job done, he felt that they should have a pin or a badge or something, so he

228
00:19:59,321 --> 00:20:04,380
had French junk sent to the Philadelphia Mint.

229
00:20:04,381 --> 00:20:09,000
And there were over 7,400 metals that were

230
00:20:09,001 --> 00:20:17,780
struck and were given to those who had served two years or more during the construction

231
00:20:17,781 --> 00:20:19,560
days.

232
00:20:19,561 --> 00:20:24,060
The medals, struck from the steel of discarded French construction equipment,

233
00:20:24,061 --> 00:20:26,560
featured a cameo of Roosevelt.

234
00:20:26,561 --> 00:20:29,560
My medal number 6726.

235
00:20:29,561 --> 00:20:35,560
There's a lot of history between number one and 6726.

236
00:20:37,560 --> 00:20:42,560
Digging the waterway through the Panamanian Mountains was a monumental feat,

237
00:20:42,561 --> 00:20:45,560
but that was only part of the construction process.

238
00:20:45,561 --> 00:20:50,940
The locks which would lift and lower ships from the elevated canal were equally mammoth structures.

239
00:20:52,040 --> 00:20:57,100
Their jobs would be to raise the world's largest ships to the height of a seven-story building.

240
00:20:58,700 --> 00:21:03,600
Built in 36-foot sections, they were the largest concrete structures on Earth.

241
00:21:03,601 --> 00:21:06,840
A thousand feet long, a hundred and ten feet wide,

242
00:21:07,180 --> 00:21:11,600
each of the six locks could hold an object the size of the Eiffel Tower.

243
00:21:11,601 --> 00:21:17,600
They were designed to fill with water flushed in from the lake through pipes the size of railroad tunnels,

244
00:21:17,601 --> 00:21:20,600
with two valves weighing 10 tons each.

245
00:21:20,601 --> 00:21:25,600
Even though the lock doors weighed 700 tons, they were so perfectly balanced

246
00:21:25,601 --> 00:21:30,600
that their opening and closing required only the power of a 40-horsepower motor,

247
00:21:30,601 --> 00:21:34,600
the same power used by many industrial lawnmowers.

248
00:21:34,601 --> 00:21:41,600
The locks operated exclusively from electricity, a radical new technology for the time, and

249
00:21:42,220 --> 00:21:48,220
their power was to be generated by Gatun Dam, a one and one half mile long earth barrier

250
00:21:48,221 --> 00:21:51,080
made from the soil of the Calabra Cut.

251
00:21:51,081 --> 00:21:55,220
Because of the enormous volume of water in the man-made

252
00:21:57,840 --> 00:22:03,720
lake, the dam is designed to process in a single day the water equivalent of two Niagara Falls.

253
00:22:03,721 --> 00:22:09,300
The Panama Canal represents a perfect balance between technology and teamwork,

254
00:22:09,301 --> 00:22:11,460
dreams and determination.

255
00:22:11,461 --> 00:22:15,760
It was born of a unique convergence of men, might and money,

256
00:22:15,761 --> 00:22:18,600
which defined a defiant age.

257
00:22:20,600 --> 00:22:21,300
Dr.

258
00:22:21,301 --> 00:22:27,640
Gorga's work on sanitation at the Panama Canal is credited with saving at least 71,000 lives

259
00:22:27,641 --> 00:22:30,140
and some 40 million workdays.

260
00:22:33,720 --> 00:22:47,720
I think I should mention what it meant to work on the Panama Canal and the actual experience of working together with a closeness that you never felt that you were

261
00:22:48,187 --> 00:22:48,887
alone.

262
00:22:49,720 --> 00:22:58,720
And if you were working with a group, if for some reason one man fell out, well then there was others right there to take on his part.

263
00:22:58,721 --> 00:23:03,160
And there was no such thing as saying, well, that's his job.

264
00:23:03,940 --> 00:23:05,140
It's our job.

265
00:23:07,840 --> 00:23:14,540
The epic nature of the Panama Canal inspired a spirit of camaraderie among the workers.

266
00:23:15,160 --> 00:23:17,740
But the project could test the strongest of men.

267
00:23:18,440 --> 00:23:21,240
In February of 1907, for personal reasons,

268
00:23:21,400 --> 00:23:25,440
Chief Engineer John Stevens resigned his post and left Panama.

269
00:23:26,220 --> 00:23:29,740
It disheartened the crews, but it enraged the president.

270
00:23:31,080 --> 00:23:34,020
President Roosevelt was set on appointing a new man

271
00:23:34,021 --> 00:23:36,660
to head the effort who could not leave his post.

272
00:23:37,180 --> 00:23:40,020
That man was George Washington Gothels.

273
00:23:40,540 --> 00:23:43,520
He was regular military, demanding, authoritative,

274
00:23:43,521 --> 00:23:46,240
and best of all, if he tried to quit,

275
00:23:46,700 --> 00:23:49,100
he could be court-martialed and sent to prison.

276
00:23:51,420 --> 00:23:53,220
Every day was a work day,

277
00:23:53,221 --> 00:24:00,260
and of course Sunday we were all supposed to observe the Sabbath,

278
00:24:01,580 --> 00:24:08,160
but there was always necessary work to be done that couldn't wait for Monday.

279
00:24:09,820 --> 00:24:13,540
The military regimen pushed the project over the top.

280
00:24:14,180 --> 00:24:18,280
In the end, 12 million cubic yards of concrete were set in the locks

281
00:24:18,281 --> 00:24:24,920
and enough dirt was hauled from the Calabra Cut to equal three and a half times the volume of the Suez Canal.

282
00:24:25,640 --> 00:24:29,919
On May 20th, 1913, Joseph Kirk in shovel 222 met D.J.

283
00:24:29,920 --> 00:24:34,440
MacDonald in shovel 230 at the bottom of the excavation.

284
00:24:34,960 --> 00:24:39,180
The digging was done. The canal was prepared to be filled.

285
00:24:40,240 --> 00:24:45,820
Four months later, workers placed the final dynamite in a small dike that held back the Chagras River.

286
00:24:45,821 --> 00:24:50,060
We were very, very proud to be successful.

287
00:24:51,300 --> 00:24:58,300
And when the canal was completed, and when they blew up the dike at Gambor,

288
00:24:59,900 --> 00:25:09,440
why, it was, I'll never forget the applause or the noise, the whistling and the yelling,

289
00:25:09,441 --> 00:25:14,320
you know, that everyone made that were standing on the banks of the canal.

290
00:25:14,321 --> 00:25:18,460
when the dike was blown up.

291
00:25:20,400 --> 00:25:23,880
The water started flowing, the locks were filled,

292
00:25:24,140 --> 00:25:27,700
and when the water level had risen, a tugboat was draped in flags

293
00:25:27,701 --> 00:25:32,800
and sent to make the first trip through the greatest engineering accomplishment of its time.

294
00:25:36,700 --> 00:25:38,760
Thousands cheered the triumph.

295
00:25:39,760 --> 00:25:42,200
But the cheering did not last long.

296
00:25:43,140 --> 00:25:45,620
By the time the steamship Ancon

297
00:25:45,621 --> 00:25:47,660
officially opened canal operations

298
00:25:47,661 --> 00:25:49,920
on August 15, 1914,

299
00:25:50,760 --> 00:25:52,900
ten years after the work had begun,

300
00:25:53,260 --> 00:25:54,820
the story of the Panama Canal

301
00:25:54,821 --> 00:25:57,540
was buried on the back pages of newspapers

302
00:25:57,541 --> 00:25:59,600
because just days before,

303
00:26:00,160 --> 00:26:01,660
Europe had erupted in war.

304
00:26:03,160 --> 00:26:05,400
At first, America was determined

305
00:26:05,401 --> 00:26:07,040
to stay out of the conflict.

306
00:26:07,560 --> 00:26:09,640
Instead of fighting, we put on a fair,

307
00:26:09,641 --> 00:26:15,120
the spectacular Panama-Pacific International Exposition in San Francisco,

308
00:26:15,860 --> 00:26:19,920
celebrating the ingenuity and magnificence of America and the canal,

309
00:26:20,080 --> 00:26:25,960
and marking the emergence of the United States as a cultural and technological world power,

310
00:26:26,420 --> 00:26:29,160
but the rest of the world didn't have time to celebrate.

311
00:26:31,620 --> 00:26:34,340
The United States finally entered the war,

312
00:26:34,640 --> 00:26:38,220
and with the passage of the Pacific Fleet through the canal in 1919,

313
00:26:38,221 --> 00:26:43,280
Teddy Roosevelt's dream of a two-ocean navy became a reality.

314
00:26:44,140 --> 00:26:46,360
But Teddy never lived to see the triumph.

315
00:26:47,340 --> 00:26:49,840
He had died a few months earlier.

316
00:26:53,640 --> 00:26:58,020
After the war, the United States disassembled a third of its battleships

317
00:26:58,021 --> 00:27:00,020
in the collective cause of world peace.

318
00:27:00,600 --> 00:27:02,740
The Navy's use of the canal dropped off,

319
00:27:03,060 --> 00:27:07,280
and a worldwide depression was having a negative impact on the canal as well.

320
00:27:08,220 --> 00:27:18,460
There were periods of time, traffic became so slow, so low, so minimal, that there were

321
00:27:18,461 --> 00:27:24,220
layoffs and I recall one time my father was working two different jobs.

322
00:27:24,221 --> 00:27:27,640
If they were laid off, they would have to go back to the states.

323
00:27:27,641 --> 00:27:28,940
And what would we do there?

324
00:27:28,941 --> 00:27:32,660
Stand on street corners selling apples and pencils?

325
00:27:32,661 --> 00:27:38,040
George Mueller and Joan Marie Ridge deGrummond are canal brats, children of canal workers

326
00:27:38,041 --> 00:27:43,220
who lived in the American zone and often grew up to join the workforce themselves.

327
00:27:43,221 --> 00:27:50,180
Against the backdrop of Panama, they developed their own way of making it through the Depression.

328
00:27:50,181 --> 00:27:55,540
These men pulled together and they decided that they would work maybe one or two weeks

329
00:27:55,541 --> 00:28:00,260
a month and that person could work one or two weeks a month and that way they could

330
00:28:00,261 --> 00:28:04,880
keep their housing and still have a job.

331
00:28:04,881 --> 00:28:07,380
But with the basic canal construction finished

332
00:28:07,381 --> 00:28:09,720
and most of the workers shipped back home,

333
00:28:09,721 --> 00:28:13,260
the American presence in Panama became a military one.

334
00:28:13,261 --> 00:28:16,880
A strong defense of the canal would become an important asset

335
00:28:16,881 --> 00:28:18,880
as the world grew more restless.

336
00:28:23,600 --> 00:28:25,740
Leonardo da Vinci should be credited

337
00:28:25,741 --> 00:28:28,980
with the earliest plan for Watergate locks.

338
00:28:28,981 --> 00:28:31,760
All modern locks follow his plan,

339
00:28:31,761 --> 00:28:34,760
even the massive structures at the Panama Canal.

340
00:28:43,640 --> 00:28:48,640
December 7, 1941, once again, war had erupted,

341
00:28:48,641 --> 00:28:52,220
and once again, sea power would be a determining factor.

342
00:28:53,880 --> 00:28:56,660
The Panama Canal would be a vulnerable linchpin

343
00:28:56,661 --> 00:28:57,920
in the Allied effort.

344
00:29:01,760 --> 00:29:05,760
Franklin Delano Roosevelt, concerned about Panama's

345
00:29:05,761 --> 00:29:09,760
strategic value, exposed Nazi designs on the waterway.

346
00:29:09,761 --> 00:29:16,760
They have divided South America into five vassal states,

347
00:29:16,761 --> 00:29:21,760
bringing the whole continent under their domination.

348
00:29:21,761 --> 00:29:26,760
And they have also so arranged it that the territory of one

349
00:29:26,761 --> 00:29:35,760
The glory of one of these new puppet states includes the Republic of Panama and our great

350
00:29:35,761 --> 00:29:39,760
lifeline, the Panama Canal.

351
00:29:39,761 --> 00:29:42,760
That is his plan.

352
00:29:42,761 --> 00:29:46,760
It will never go into effect.

353
00:29:46,761 --> 00:29:48,365
In April 1941, the U.S.

354
00:29:48,366 --> 00:29:52,760
Coast Guard seized several Axis ships to prevent the widespread

355
00:29:52,761 --> 00:29:54,760
a widespread Nazi sabotage plot,

356
00:29:54,761 --> 00:29:58,760
which included the scuffling of vessels in the canal.

357
00:29:58,761 --> 00:30:01,760
The fear was rooted in reality,

358
00:30:01,761 --> 00:30:04,760
for Germany, in conquering its weaker European neighbors,

359
00:30:04,761 --> 00:30:07,760
had gained control of some of their colonial footholds

360
00:30:07,761 --> 00:30:10,760
in the Western Hemisphere.

361
00:30:10,761 --> 00:30:14,760
This put the canal within range of air attack.

362
00:30:16,760 --> 00:30:19,760
To protect against attack and sabotage,

363
00:30:19,761 --> 00:30:30,212
The canal instituted a top-secret program called SIP-7, an emergency damming method in which an underwater blockade could be lifted hydraulically to hold back the

364
00:30:30,213 --> 00:30:35,180
raging waters of Gatun Lake if the doors of the locks were hit by Nazi bombs.

365
00:30:36,000 --> 00:30:48,260
Should a gate be damaged by some wartime activity, they could easily lose all the water in Gatun Lake, and that was one of their concerns.

366
00:30:48,261 --> 00:30:53,860
It could be activated in minutes, even by hand, if all electricity were cut off.

367
00:30:54,360 --> 00:30:58,420
SIP-7 proved to be an integral part of America's wartime defense,

368
00:30:58,780 --> 00:31:02,240
but the canal had another unusual influence on the war effort.

369
00:31:03,920 --> 00:31:08,540
Around the globe, American fighting men were suddenly encountering rare tropical diseases.

370
00:31:09,260 --> 00:31:13,720
What had been learned about controlling malaria during the construction of the canal

371
00:31:13,721 --> 00:31:17,420
was now taught to GIs through educational cartoons.

372
00:31:18,260 --> 00:31:24,800
This is the story of an outcast, now a hunted wanderer over the face of the globe.

373
00:31:24,801 --> 00:31:32,860
You all know her name, Anopheles Annie, the malaria mosquito.

374
00:31:40,140 --> 00:31:45,700
To look at me now you wouldn't hardly believe it. But back in the good old days

375
00:31:45,701 --> 00:31:48,700
I was really some stuff, yeah?

376
00:31:48,701 --> 00:31:51,700
I took my drink strength,

377
00:31:51,701 --> 00:31:55,700
but the boys got theirs mixed with a Mickey Finn.

378
00:32:03,700 --> 00:32:06,700
As a facility and as a symbol of a nation's might,

379
00:32:06,701 --> 00:32:10,700
the Panama Canal had established its importance.

380
00:32:10,701 --> 00:32:13,700
But there were some people who forgot that despite the money,

381
00:32:13,701 --> 00:32:19,280
blood, sweat, and tears the United States had invested in the 10-mile strip, Panama still

382
00:32:19,281 --> 00:32:21,474
belonged to the Panamanians.

383
00:32:21,475 --> 00:32:26,960
There was an enforced segregation between Americans and the Panamanian

384
00:32:26,961 --> 00:32:27,664
people.

385
00:32:27,665 --> 00:32:36,620
If you're not an American citizen living in the Canal Zone, you are expected to be out of

386
00:32:36,621 --> 00:32:41,500
the Canal Zone after dark and evening, unless you had business.

387
00:32:41,501 --> 00:32:51,200
Now many of us had domestic servants, and they could stay several hours after sunset,

388
00:32:51,201 --> 00:32:55,460
and they would not be challenged as they were returning to their own homes in Panama or

389
00:32:55,461 --> 00:32:58,300
coming back into the zone in the early morning hours.

390
00:32:58,301 --> 00:33:03,820
One thing about the Canal Zone, they insisted on employees, the parents, that is, with their

391
00:33:03,821 --> 00:33:07,557
families return to the states periodically.

392
00:33:07,558 --> 00:33:11,380
The idea being that they wanted the families

393
00:33:11,381 --> 00:33:16,940
to remain Americanized rather than to assimilate too much of the Panamanian culture to the

394
00:33:16,941 --> 00:33:21,700
extent that they would forget their identity as Americans.

395
00:33:21,701 --> 00:33:27,569
As Panama grew into the 20th century, Panamanian nationalism increased.

396
00:33:27,570 --> 00:33:29,140
In 1964, the United

397
00:33:29,141 --> 00:33:33,867
States refused to allow the Panamanian flag to be raised alongside the U.S.

398
00:33:33,868 --> 00:33:34,568
flag in the

399
00:33:34,569 --> 00:33:35,408
Canal Zone.

400
00:33:35,409 --> 00:33:41,500
Enraged citizens took their anger to the streets in a violent riot that claimed

401
00:33:41,501 --> 00:33:47,760
the lives of 22 Panamanians and four U.S. soldiers and injured more than 350 people.

402
00:33:48,880 --> 00:33:49,874
Many in the U.S.

403
00:33:49,875 --> 00:33:54,100
blamed a subversive communist element for inflaming the Panamanians,

404
00:33:54,101 --> 00:33:59,580
but the Panamanian government didn't agree that nationalism was subversive

405
00:33:59,581 --> 00:34:03,920
and immediately severed diplomatic relations with the United States.

406
00:34:04,520 --> 00:34:10,020
The Organization of American States called an emergency meeting to urge negotiation.

407
00:34:10,420 --> 00:34:16,600
How would you feel if France exercised jurisdiction over a swath of land 10 miles wide

408
00:34:16,601 --> 00:34:20,960
along the banks of the Mississippi River from New Orleans to Canada?

409
00:34:20,961 --> 00:34:31,360
Nothing in the 1903 treaty or its amendments indicates that Panama ever ceded the canal zone to the United States.

410
00:34:32,160 --> 00:34:39,160
So President Johnson promised to renegotiate the treaty of 1903 and recognize the sovereignty of the isthmus.

411
00:34:39,740 --> 00:34:48,780
That was the first stage in a process that would eventually turn control of the waterway over to the country that had been created so the Panama Canal could be built.

412
00:34:50,960 --> 00:34:56,360
Three years after he left the presidency, Theodore Roosevelt made his boastful,

413
00:34:56,361 --> 00:34:59,057
I took the Isthmus statement, revealing the U.S.

414
00:34:59,058 --> 00:35:01,360
involvement in the Panamanian Revolution.

415
00:35:02,060 --> 00:35:06,760
This public revolution so angered the Colombian government that they demanded and received

416
00:35:06,761 --> 00:35:09,700
$25 million in reparations from the U.S.

417
00:35:15,700 --> 00:35:19,600
The economic advantages of the Panama Canal are obvious.

418
00:35:19,601 --> 00:35:25,460
Its unique geography facilitates trade with a direct water link between Europe and the Pacific

419
00:35:25,461 --> 00:35:31,360
and trims nearly 8,000 miles off the shipping route between New York and San Francisco.

420
00:35:36,040 --> 00:35:39,820
The engineering behind this modern marvel is ingenious,

421
00:35:40,400 --> 00:35:43,400
and the transitive ship's a masterpiece of choreography.

422
00:35:44,320 --> 00:35:47,980
The route begins on either the Atlantic or Pacific entrance

423
00:35:47,981 --> 00:35:51,400
where vessels make their request to canal traffic control

424
00:35:51,401 --> 00:35:53,900
for permission to pass through the locks.

425
00:35:55,680 --> 00:35:57,960
The locks are double-sided,

426
00:35:58,140 --> 00:36:02,040
permitting traffic to flow in either or both directions at once.

427
00:36:03,540 --> 00:36:05,960
Experienced canal pilots board the ships

428
00:36:05,961 --> 00:36:08,620
to navigate them through the narrow waterways,

429
00:36:09,160 --> 00:36:10,640
helped into the locks themselves

430
00:36:10,641 --> 00:36:13,660
by special electric locomotives called mules,

431
00:36:14,180 --> 00:36:15,940
which pull the ships into the chambers.

432
00:36:16,800 --> 00:36:22,340
Except for the opening and closing of the massive locked doors and the towing of ships into the locked chambers,

433
00:36:22,500 --> 00:36:26,900
the entire raising and lowering operation works from gravity.

434
00:36:28,640 --> 00:36:32,640
Panama's jungles receive 100 inches of rainfall each year

435
00:36:32,641 --> 00:36:39,300
and flush billions of pounds of water from the high regions of the Continental Divide to the Atlantic and Pacific.

436
00:36:40,000 --> 00:36:43,280
As the water fills the locked chambers on its way to the sea,

437
00:36:43,281 --> 00:36:47,200
ships float on its surface and rise without mechanical assistance.

438
00:36:51,280 --> 00:36:58,000
What is mind-boggling about the operation is its overwhelming size, which can best be

439
00:36:58,001 --> 00:37:02,451
appreciated when the locks are drained for maintenance.

440
00:37:02,452 --> 00:37:04,960
Repair and upkeep is an ongoing

441
00:37:04,961 --> 00:37:09,600
demand with over 100 million dollars spent in the last 10 years on improvements.

442
00:37:09,601 --> 00:37:16,600
The difficult handwork is accomplished in largely the same fashion as in 1915.

443
00:37:16,601 --> 00:37:21,600
A human touch is required on every square inch of the locks.

444
00:37:27,600 --> 00:37:29,645
The lock chambers are massive.

445
00:37:29,646 --> 00:37:33,600
Their length longer than three regulation football fields.

446
00:37:33,601 --> 00:37:37,600
Their volume the equivalent of nearly 500 school buses.

447
00:37:37,601 --> 00:37:42,600
Four space shuttles could be parked within each of the locks.

448
00:37:42,601 --> 00:37:45,600
When these giant chambers were first designed,

449
00:37:45,601 --> 00:37:49,600
they could easily accommodate three ocean-worthy vessels at a time.

450
00:37:49,601 --> 00:37:53,600
Today, shipbuilders base the dimensions of their Panamax cargo ships

451
00:37:53,601 --> 00:37:59,600
so that one enormous craft can perfectly match the contours of the lock.

452
00:37:59,601 --> 00:38:03,600
Once through the lock, ships enter the man-made Gatun Lake

453
00:38:03,601 --> 00:38:06,600
formed by the waters of the Chagras River.

454
00:38:06,601 --> 00:38:12,540
The lake also contains hundreds of tiny islands with ecological microsystems,

455
00:38:12,541 --> 00:38:17,360
which are natural laboratories for valuable research by universities and scientists.

456
00:38:18,500 --> 00:38:21,460
But the lake does not extend from one coast to the other.

457
00:38:21,940 --> 00:38:27,560
The Calabra Cut meanders for nine miles through the mountains of the Continental Divide to link the system.

458
00:38:28,840 --> 00:38:32,600
Carving this piece of the puzzle was the deadliest part of the canal construction,

459
00:38:32,601 --> 00:38:38,100
as unstable hills avalanched into the work area to bury men and equipment.

460
00:38:42,500 --> 00:38:48,660
Even today, the most intensive canal maintenance efforts are focused on the treacherous Calabra Cut.

461
00:38:49,200 --> 00:38:54,660
Since its original construction, this channel has been widened from 300 to 500 feet

462
00:38:54,661 --> 00:38:57,600
to allow passage of wider modern vessels.

463
00:38:57,601 --> 00:39:06,820
more earth has been removed in this explosive process of widening than was excavated originally

464
00:39:06,821 --> 00:39:18,120
to build the canal the enormity of contemporary ships contributes to the erosion of the canal's

465
00:39:18,121 --> 00:39:24,000
narrow banks as the churning of their giant propellers scour the canal's shallow base

466
00:39:24,001 --> 00:39:25,300
dislodging silt.

467
00:39:26,000 --> 00:39:30,340
More than 500 men labor 365 days a year

468
00:39:30,341 --> 00:39:32,820
in a constant dredging operation

469
00:39:32,821 --> 00:39:37,280
to remove 2.5 million cubic yards of soil each year.

470
00:39:38,440 --> 00:39:41,500
The sludge is pumped inland to drain and dry,

471
00:39:41,501 --> 00:39:45,640
but the Panamanian weather often sabotages that effort.

472
00:39:48,320 --> 00:39:50,580
At 9 degrees above the equator,

473
00:39:50,720 --> 00:39:53,100
the tropical sun bakes a thin crust

474
00:39:53,101 --> 00:39:59,300
on the surface of the soil, keeping the thick glutinous soup protected underneath, unable to drain.

475
00:40:01,000 --> 00:40:07,240
During the rainy season, water collects in the cracks and becomes a breeding ground for disease-carrying mosquitoes.

476
00:40:08,840 --> 00:40:12,480
These crusts must routinely be broken for drainage,

477
00:40:12,481 --> 00:40:19,220
but the weight of men and equipment would easily crack the surface and cause them to be sucked into the slime.

478
00:40:19,221 --> 00:40:27,400
The solution is called explosive ditching, a process in which men race across the pits to

479
00:40:27,401 --> 00:40:33,140
deposit dynamite charges, then blast away channels in the crust to allow standing water to drain back

480
00:40:33,141 --> 00:40:36,794
into the canal. The results are immediate.

481
00:40:36,795 --> 00:40:41,580
Blasts are monitored with special high-speed cameras so

482
00:40:41,581 --> 00:40:45,680
the shock waves from the explosions can be measured from a safe distance and studied.

483
00:40:49,220 --> 00:41:00,740
As in the original construction of the canal, a massive full-time crew of more than 8,000

484
00:41:00,741 --> 00:41:06,540
people are constantly blasting, digging and dredging to maintain the canal.

485
00:41:06,541 --> 00:41:18,520
A project like the Panama Canal can no longer be built.

486
00:41:18,780 --> 00:41:22,700
In fact, it would be mind-boggling to assume that that project

487
00:41:22,701 --> 00:41:26,340
could get through all the environmental hurdles that are necessary

488
00:41:26,341 --> 00:41:33,120
in order to have a project like that proposed, circulated, and built to completion.

489
00:41:33,920 --> 00:41:37,800
When Teddy Roosevelt bullied the Panama Canal into existence,

490
00:41:38,080 --> 00:41:40,560
he didn't have to contend with the legal challenges

491
00:41:40,561 --> 00:41:44,540
which would today prevent the first shovel from ever breaking earth.

492
00:41:45,100 --> 00:41:49,820
In that sense, the canal is the last hurrah for the kind of ambitious project

493
00:41:49,821 --> 00:41:52,360
which would dare to change the shape of the world.

494
00:41:52,880 --> 00:41:57,420
Yet the waterway has found a permanent place in the imagination of men.

495
00:41:58,320 --> 00:42:02,020
Over half a million ships have made the nine-hour voyage from sea to sea

496
00:42:02,021 --> 00:42:05,660
over the continental divide spanned by the Panama Canal.

497
00:42:08,460 --> 00:42:12,480
The operation is funded by tolls for using the locks,

498
00:42:13,000 --> 00:42:17,520
ranging from $142,000 for majestic cruise ships

499
00:42:17,521 --> 00:42:22,460
to 38 cents for one daring man who swam the distance.

500
00:42:23,740 --> 00:42:27,880
But the real value of the canal cannot be measured in dollars.

501
00:42:27,881 --> 00:42:32,880
The Panama Canal is a working monument to man's nature,

502
00:42:32,881 --> 00:42:36,880
at times stubborn and harsh, but always longing for greatness

503
00:42:36,881 --> 00:42:40,880
and cradled in bittersweet glory.

504
00:42:40,881 --> 00:42:43,880
As we pass through the canal during the day, we get to a spot

505
00:42:43,881 --> 00:42:48,880
which is the deepest cut. It's called the Gallard Cut.

506
00:42:48,881 --> 00:42:51,880
And that was the scene of the biggest digging

507
00:42:51,881 --> 00:42:55,880
and of course biggest casualties while they were digging through.

508
00:42:55,881 --> 00:43:00,600
through and as we go through there's a big plaque on the side of the hill to

509
00:43:00,601 --> 00:43:07,340
commemorate all the lives that were lost during the construction. The thousands of

510
00:43:07,341 --> 00:43:12,680
workers who sacrificed their lives when this narrow sliver of land was wrenched

511
00:43:12,681 --> 00:43:18,780
from nature were also building connections between men and creating a

512
00:43:18,781 --> 00:43:25,020
door which would lead the world into the 20th century.

513
00:43:25,021 --> 00:43:29,000
It is the most important part of my education.

514
00:43:29,001 --> 00:43:33,100
I learned things there that cannot be written,

515
00:43:33,101 --> 00:43:36,800
some that you can't learn from textbooks,

516
00:43:36,801 --> 00:43:39,140
and no one can teach you.

517
00:43:39,141 --> 00:43:45,340
You must participate and observe and work with people.

518
00:43:45,341 --> 00:43:50,420
I wish now I had stayed, but that's another story.

